WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:03.462
Venomous snakes have long held our imaginations
in thrall.

00:00:03.462 --> 00:00:09.040
And it makes sense: to this day, humans fall
prey to their natural defenses.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:14.389
Secretive, slithering, seductive, and scary,
the serpent has claimed a prominent place

00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:18.180
in our literature, art, religion, even natural
history.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:23.160
And there are few serpents more frightening
or pervasive than the king of snakes: the

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.270
mighty basilisk.

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:29.230
I’m sure for some of you when you see this
picture, you’ll say—oh wait, that’s

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:30.280
a cockatrice.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:31.870
Well…you’re not wrong.

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:37.570
That’s right, the elusive and terrifying
snake monster and the fiery rooster monster

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:44.270
not only share origins, but are arguably the
same monster, just with two different names.

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:48.580
This , this, and this, are all the same creature.

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:50.390
Technically, even this little one.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:52.600
But we’ll get to that cutie later.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:58.030
Join me on an exploration of the basilisk-cockatrice
connection—and how it went from frightening

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:02.270
snake to a metaphor for pure evil.

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:06.659
[Monstrum intro.]

00:01:06.659 --> 00:01:11.420
The earliest references to this serpentine
monster date back thousands of years, to the

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:12.570
1st century.

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:18.070
In his epic poem Pharsalia, the Roman poet
Marcus Annaeus Lucanus, known in English as

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:24.390
Lucan, introduced us to a serpent capable
of producing immense terror, writing “The

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:29.740
Basilisk which pours forth, hisses terrifying
all beasts, which harms before its poison

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:34.820
and orders the entire crowd far out of its
way and on the empty sand is king.”

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:39.900
With the stroke of his pen, Lucan brought
to life a terrifying snake whose royal status

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:42.140
was firmly established from the beginning.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:47.060
So lethal, the Basilisk’s venom can kill
even after the beast has been destroyed, as

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:51.970
the soldier in Pharsalia learns when he stabs
the snake and the venom runs up the spear

00:01:51.970 --> 00:01:53.119
into his hand.

00:01:53.119 --> 00:01:58.310
In a moment of foresight, the man slices off
his own hand with a sword to save his life.

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:03.760
Pliny’s encyclopaedic Historia Naturalis--
or “Natural History” -- contains a similar

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:09.360
story, only this time both the rider and his
horse die after the basilisk is speared.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:16.020
In this text, the blackish-yellow, more-than-foot-long
snake slithers along with its upper body erect,

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:19.819
hypnotizing its prey with eyes sunken into
a sharp head.

00:02:19.819 --> 00:02:24.970
According to Pliny, the basilisk can kill
with its gaze alone, as well as with its noxious

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:25.970
breath.

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:30.760
Interestingly, we also learn of the monster’s
weakness—the stench of a weasel will kill

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:34.190
the basilisk, although the weasel too will
perish.

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:38.080
Pliny asserts that the basilisk earns its
Greek name, basiliskos [vasiliskos; βασιλίσκος]

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:43.569
and Latin equivalent basiliscus—both meaning
“kinglet”— for a crown- like white spot

00:02:43.569 --> 00:02:46.420
on its sharp head resembling a diadem.

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:51.840
In fact, those two early texts -- Pharsalia
and Historia Naturalis -- laid the foundation

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:56.300
for the attributes that have been associated
with the basilisk ever since: that it is a

00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:02.780
venomous snake whose breath, gaze, or even
hiss could destroy all life—animal, human,

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:03.830
or vegetable.

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:08.700
Moreover, the origin stories of the basilisk
include mention of an ability to summon other

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:13.610
snakes with its hiss even as it frightens
off or kills all other creatures.

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:18.879
It’s unsurprising then that this powerful,
decorated snake with its crown-like spot would

00:03:18.879 --> 00:03:23.239
be subsequently called “the king of the
serpents” in the Greek poet Nicander’s

00:03:23.239 --> 00:03:24.239
Theriaca.

00:03:24.239 --> 00:03:28.409
Authors and artists in the Medieval period
seem to have taken liberty with the “kinglet,”

00:03:28.409 --> 00:03:32.240
depicting the creature with a crown or even
a rooster’s comb.

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:36.930
Beginning in the 13th century, we find texts
describing a “reptilian monster,” and

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:42.420
even the “king of serpents” with all of
the traits of the basilisk plus a new one:

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:47.970
a rooster’s comb rather than a crown adorns
its head, and it bears a new name — the

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:48.970
cockatrice.

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:53.420
Over time we see the names “basilisk”
and the “cockatrice” used interchangeably

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:57.400
as the creature becomes more and more of a
snake-bird hybrid.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:02.210
The cockatrice appears in literature and art,
in marginalia and coat of arms.

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:07.239
Bearing a combination of chicken and reptilian
features (with dragon wings or an extra head

00:04:07.239 --> 00:04:12.110
occasionally thrown in there for good measure),
it is said to petrify with its gaze or a touch

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:13.269
of its beak.

00:04:13.269 --> 00:04:18.199
With all this fantastic and suggestive imagery
around its appearance, it’s no surprise

00:04:18.199 --> 00:04:21.919
that religions would embrace its wealth of
allegorical power.

00:04:21.919 --> 00:04:26.439
In the Christian tradition, the serpent was
already associated with sin and the Devil,

00:04:26.439 --> 00:04:30.349
due largely to its seductive role in the fall
of the Garden of Eden.

00:04:30.349 --> 00:04:35.020
So the basilisk a.k.a. the cockatrice didn’t
really stand a chance.

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:40.360
Take for example an origin story of the basilisk
that emerged in the 1100s, which proposed

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:43.349
that it is born from an egg hatched by a toad.

00:04:43.349 --> 00:04:47.689
The first person to suggest this was St. Hildegard
in her book Physica.

00:04:47.689 --> 00:04:52.810
She even goes as far as to say it is the “diabolic
art of the ancient serpent” that comes from

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:56.119
“the Antichrist” that spawns this unnatural
hatching.

00:04:56.119 --> 00:05:00.900
Over time, these dubious origins became part
of the basilisk and the cockatrice myths,

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:05.879
evolving even further to include details like
the egg is laid by a rooster and that it would

00:05:05.879 --> 00:05:07.839
be incubated in a dung heap....

00:05:07.839 --> 00:05:13.149
The basilisk became not just dangerous and
poisonous, but a literal embodiment of evil

00:05:13.149 --> 00:05:15.449
that must be vanquished.

00:05:15.449 --> 00:05:20.069
In fact, a lot of art from the Medieval period
shows Christ, a saint, or some other holy

00:05:20.069 --> 00:05:22.729
figure defeating the basilisk or cockatrice.

00:05:22.729 --> 00:05:28.180
But it can still be killed by a weasel, which
explains these absolutely delightful illustrations.

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:33.559
And of course, given the shift toward religious
allegory in the history of the serpent beast,

00:05:33.559 --> 00:05:38.589
we see the power of prayer taking hold as
another defense against its evil effects.

00:05:38.589 --> 00:05:43.430
A bit confusingly, around this time we learn
that carrying a rooster around can also protect

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:47.240
you—because the rooster’s crow can frighten
the basilisk to death.

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:51.979
Or, if you’re quick on your feet, spotting
the basilisk before it spots you, or showing

00:05:51.979 --> 00:05:55.139
it its own reflection are among your defensive
options.

00:05:55.139 --> 00:06:00.180
Oh yes, the idea that humans could survive
the deadly gaze of the basilisk by looking

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:02.779
at it in a mirror had begun to take hold.

00:06:02.779 --> 00:06:07.339
So apparently, Harry Potter got that part
correct, even though Voldemort’s ‘basilisk’

00:06:07.339 --> 00:06:13.009
Nagini is much, much, much larger than any
other depiction of a basilisk in literature

00:06:13.009 --> 00:06:14.009
or art.

00:06:14.009 --> 00:06:15.189
That’s artistic license for you!

00:06:15.189 --> 00:06:19.499
Interestingly, the deadly gaze of the monster,
along with its more negative associations

00:06:19.499 --> 00:06:24.699
with sin and evil in Christianity, eventually
led to its allegorical use to indicate both

00:06:24.699 --> 00:06:29.680
“vile scoundrels” and dangerously beautiful
and seductive women in the 17th century and

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:30.960
beyond.

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:35.339
Which likely goes back to the portrayal of
Eve in the Garden of Eden, although there

00:06:35.339 --> 00:06:40.080
many other plausible contributing factors
including the myths of Medusa and the Lamia,

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:42.169
along with you know—patriarchy.

00:06:42.169 --> 00:06:47.389
John Dryden, famous English poet and playwright
of the 17th century was one such author.

00:06:47.389 --> 00:06:52.749
The queen in The Spanish Frya, or the Double
Discovery is called the Basilisk of Torrismond

00:06:52.749 --> 00:06:54.210
since she “kills…with her Eyes.”

00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:57.770
But let’s go back to those early origins
of the basilisk.

00:06:57.770 --> 00:07:02.229
There are scholars who argue that the basilisk
of ancient lore was actually a real snake

00:07:02.229 --> 00:07:07.479
that, with time and the human imagination,
was imbued with more and more traits until

00:07:07.479 --> 00:07:12.740
it became something utterly fantastical, and
completely divorced from its mundane roots.

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:17.909
If it is true, as some assert, that the early
basilisk at one point believed to be native

00:07:17.909 --> 00:07:20.169
to Libya, this makes sense.

00:07:20.169 --> 00:07:25.990
After all, Libya is home to the Egyptian cobra,
one of the most terrifying snakes on the planet.

00:07:25.990 --> 00:07:30.479
Cobras are also illustrated in Egyptian hieroglyphics
with the middle of the body raised up —in

00:07:30.479 --> 00:07:35.569
a manner that resembles bird feet, which might
have later contributed to the cockatrice interpretation

00:07:35.569 --> 00:07:40.189
of the monster—and which calls to mind Pliny’s
description of the basilisk’s erect upper

00:07:40.189 --> 00:07:41.189
body.

00:07:41.189 --> 00:07:45.939
While the Egyptian cobra is a perfectly serviceable
real-life origin snake for our evil serpent,

00:07:45.939 --> 00:07:48.999
it is not the only candidate for the honor.

00:07:48.999 --> 00:07:49.999
Another theory?

00:07:49.999 --> 00:07:52.229
I offer you: the humble grass snake.

00:07:52.229 --> 00:07:55.959
The grass snake (Natrix natrix) has a yellow,
orange, or white -collar, and its defensive

00:07:55.959 --> 00:08:00.800
mechanisms include “aggressively hissing,”
secreting a foul-smelling substance from the

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:03.360
anal glands, and even playing dead.

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:07.639
Grass snakes also happen to love to hatch
their eggs in rotting wood, plant debris,

00:08:07.639 --> 00:08:09.159
compost, and dung heaps.

00:08:09.159 --> 00:08:15.989
What’s most interesting about the grass-snake-as-real-basilisk
theory is that grass snakes, while totally

00:08:15.989 --> 00:08:21.080
common, are rarely mentioned in ancient bestiaries
and early animal “encyclopedias.”

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:26.559
Meanwhile, the fictional basilisk is identified
as a “species” in almost every one of

00:08:26.559 --> 00:08:27.960
these resources.

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:32.990
This might indicate that the grass snake was
at one point misidentified as a basilisk before

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:37.360
the latter’s evolution to the cockatrice
blurred the lines between reality and fiction

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:38.360
even more.

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:43.979
To make this even more interesting, the cock’s
egg origins of the basilisk-slash cockatrice

00:08:43.979 --> 00:08:48.889
could be explained by the perfectly normal
phenomenons of hens occasionally laying yolkless

00:08:48.889 --> 00:08:54.649
eggs or hens acting like roosters because
of a hormone imbalance or an unusually dominant

00:08:54.649 --> 00:08:55.649
personality.

00:08:55.649 --> 00:09:00.329
Then there’s the occasional appearance of
parasitic worms in eggs—which will sometimes

00:09:00.329 --> 00:09:02.279
look like tiny snakes.

00:09:02.279 --> 00:09:07.370
Whether or not the basilisk aka the cockatrice
was truly based on a real animal is unclear.

00:09:07.370 --> 00:09:13.040
What we do know is that we humans have been
fascinated with the idea of the basilisk for

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:14.180
centuries.

00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:17.319
So much so that they have permeated into our
literature and art.

00:09:17.319 --> 00:09:22.920
To this day, the basilisk and cockatrice live
on in books, D&amp;D, video games, and film.

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:28.509
And coming full circle from its vaguely reptilian
associations, there are real, modern lizards

00:09:28.509 --> 00:09:31.290
named after this fantastic beast.

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:32.700
Four species of lizards, in fact.

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:39.240
They are in the Iguanidae family in the genus
Basiliscus, better known as casquehead lizards.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:44.950
Their common names include the brown or striped
basilisk, the western basilisk, the common

00:09:44.950 --> 00:09:49.390
basilisk, and the plumed lizard—whom you
may also recognize as the “Jesus lizards”

00:09:49.390 --> 00:09:51.560
for their ability to run across water.

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:56.680
This seems particularly ironic given the basilisk’s
one-time association with the Devil.

00:09:56.680 --> 00:10:00.393
While these lizards have no powers that allow
them to cause death with a glance, they do

00:10:00.393 --> 00:10:05.120
have a long tail and an elongated head that
looks like a rooster’s comb.

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:08.650
Just another way monster history infiltrates
our real world.

00:10:08.650 --> 00:10:13.410
The basilisk and the cockatrice have thrived
in our imaginations for a very long time.

00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:18.269
From king of the serpents to sin incarnate
to a metaphor for the exaggerated dangers

00:10:18.269 --> 00:10:23.610
of a woman’s allure, these serpentine/avian
monsters both fascinate and repel—their

00:10:23.610 --> 00:10:28.650
lethal gaze lingering from the pages of antiquity
into our modern world.

00:10:28.650 --> 00:10:32.129
Have you ever wondered what makes words, well,
words?

00:10:32.129 --> 00:10:37.220
Otherwords, PBS’ newest show about language
and linguistics, has you covered.

00:10:37.220 --> 00:10:42.430
Otherwords dives into languages around the
world to uncover the fascinating, unexpected,

00:10:42.430 --> 00:10:46.920
and sometimes weird stories behind the human
trait we all take for granted.

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:49.080
Check out Otherwords, right here on Storied.

